<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbasid Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abbasids, Abbasid dynasty, Abbasid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> was the Islamic caliphate that replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in 750 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, which it founded as its capital. Its early centuries were a golden age of trade, scholarship and science, but its political authority later fragmented among regional dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and Fatimid rivals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258">Mongol sack of Baghdad</a> in 1258 ended its rule there, and its legacy shaped many later Islamic empires, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate.jpg" alt="Abbasid Caliphate" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ec.Domnowall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbasid_banner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
